                            <h5>Radiotherapy planning</h5>
                            <p>
                                Radiotherapy planning is the preparatory phase where a multidisciplinary team—comprising radiation oncologists, physicists, and dosimetrists—designs a customized treatment strategy. It ensures that targeted radiation doses precisely destroy tumor cells while minimizing exposure to surrounding healthy tissues and vital organs.The planning process consists of several standard, highly calibrated stages:Simulation Scan: You will undergo a dedicated CT or MRI scan in the exact position you will be treated. This step maps your anatomy and is often combined with immobilization devices like mesh masks or body molds to ensure absolute stillness.Contouring: The clinical team maps out the Gross Tumor Volume (GTV), areas at risk for microscopic spread (CTV), and adjacent healthy Organs at Risk (OAR).Dosimetry & Optimization: Medical physicists use specialized planning software (such as the Varian Eclipse Treatment Planning System or RayStation) to calculate the optimal beam angles, intensities, and dosage.Quality Assurance (QA): Before treatment begins, the specific plan is rigorously tested on a machine to ensure it meets both safety and delivery accuracy standards.For a deeper look into the clinical process and patient positioning protocols, review the comprehensive guide provided by the Mayo Clinic. If you are preparing for a specific planning session, resources like the Cancer Research UK External Radiotherapy Planner detail what to expect on the day of your appointment.

                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>


                <div class="col-lg-6 col-md-6 mt-3" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="450">
